   "html_text": "Books 2 to 4 focus on the relation between God and the world. The general problem is whether God’s knowledge is limited to necessary states of affairs or extends to the domain of contingency as well. If the former, then God could not be said to have knowledge of humans, and so divine providence would not be efficacious. But if God does know contingents—in particular, future contingent events—then it would appear that human freedom is curtailed by God’s prior knowledge of human actions. The probl",
